CommitDate: Thu Sep 10 19:01:13 2026 +0200

    Feed `--pre` the raw ciphertext text, whitespace and all
    
    `ra sweep`'s `--pre` axis is documented -- in its own `--help` and in a comment
    inside `Ctx::stage` -- to run against the raw ciphertext text, above the initial
    decode. It was fed the WHITESPACE-STRIPPED text instead.
    
    A stripped transcription is a single token, so `reverse_words` had nothing to
    reorder and silently *was* `identity`. Any sweep naming it enumerated one
    pipeline under two labels and published a negative it had never tested. rev7 is
    the case in point: `identity` and `reverse_words` staged byte-identical streams,
    so a three-value `--pre` axis covered two points while claiming three.
    
    `Ctx::compact` becomes `Ctx::text` and carries the transcription verbatim. The
    subtlety is the variant masks: `VariantSpace::positions` indexes the *compacted*
    text, so splicing a mask into raw text naively would shift every offset and
    corrupt TG4's 128 readings -- a far worse bug than the one being fixed. Hence
    `VariantSpace::apply_preserving_whitespace`, which substitutes at the k-th
    NON-whitespace character and leaves whitespace where it found it. Stripping its
    output reproduces `apply` exactly, over every mask, which is asserted.
    
    Both production sites now go through one helper, `sweep_input_text`: the sweep
    driver and the claim recompute path each rebuilt their own stripped copy, and
    fixing only one would have made a replayed claim disagree with the sweep that
    produced it -- nondeterminism, which is harder to diagnose than the original bug.
    
    Also adds a degeneracy guard, because the real failure was not "whitespace got
    stripped" but "a named axis became a no-op and nobody was told". Each `--pre`
    and `--xf` value's staged input is hashed against the REAL target's bytes (an
    axis can be correct in general and identity on the one ciphertext under attack),
    and colliding values are named on stdout and recorded in the claim as side
    metadata -- never inside a merkle leaf, which is left byte-for-byte unchanged.
    It warns rather than errors: `stripws+reverse_words` really is the identity, and
    only the analyst can say whether a given collision is legitimate.
    
    Verified: TG4's 128 readings reproduce their merkle root and coverage digest
    byte-identically (34f4b48d..., sha256:0fce6585...); rev7's three `--pre` values
    now stage three distinct streams, with `reverse_words` giving `1A47F 294E1 ...`
    (last group first, interiors intact) against `reverse`'s `F74A1 1E492 ...`.
    
